<!-- Figures: the figures of Examples I and II show only the graphs of the -->
<!-- boundary curves (no shading, strips or end lines): the student shades -->
<!-- the region R and draws a strip.  The strip video, the tetrahedron,    -->
<!-- the reverse-order picture, the symmetry picture of Method 2, the area -->
<!-- region, and the two GeoGebra/PreFigure statements of the Concept      -->
<!-- Check are kept, with the captions and applet instructions that spelled -->
<!-- out an answer (the symmetry argument, the shadow region of Figure (4), -->
<!-- the diameter of Figure (5)) trimmed.  The base of the tetrahedron,    -->
<!-- which is the region of integration the student has to find, is an    -->
<!-- empty grid to sketch on.                                              -->
<section xml:id="section-double-integrals-general-regions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Double Integrals Over General Regions and Area by Double Integration</title>

<p>
Here we will generalize our last session's discussion about double
integrals and how they can be used to evaluate volumes. Last session we
restricted ourselves to shapes with rectangular base. Today we will
look at more general shapes for the base.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-general-regions-case1">
<title>Case I: Regions of the Form <m>R = \{(x,y) : a \le x \le b,\ f_2(x) \le y \le f_1(x)\}</m></title>

<example xml:id="example-case1-vertical">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the following double integral,
</p>

<me>
\iint_R \left(2xy + 1\right)\,dx\,dy
</me>

<p>
where
<m>R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 1,\ (-2x^2+2x) \le y \le (x^3+1)\}</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,12.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for computing the double integral of Example I.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-case1-region-prefigure">
<caption>
The graphs of <m>f_1(x) = x^3 + 1</m> and <m>f_2(x) = -2x^2 + 2x</m>.
Shade the region <m>R</m> of <xref ref="example-case1-vertical"/> on
them, and draw a typical strip.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>
The graphs of an upper and a lower curve, with the region between them left to shade.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-case1-region">
<diagram dimensions="(380,300)" margins="10">
<definition>f1(t) = (t, t^3 + 1)</definition>
<definition>f2(t) = (t, -2*t^2 + 2*t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.4,1.45,2.45)">
<axes at="c1-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="c1-f1" function="f1" domain="(-0.15,1.1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="c1-f2" function="f2" domain="(-0.15,1.1)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="c1-f1-label" anchor="(0.42,1.35)" alignment="nw"
    color="#0072B2"><m>f_1(x) = x^3 + 1</m></label>
<label at="c1-f2-label" anchor="(0.52,0.28)" alignment="s"
    color="#D55E00"><m>f_2(x) = -2x^2 + 2x</m></label>
<point at="c1-x0" p="(0,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>0</m>
</point>
<point at="c1-x1" p="(1,0)" fill="black" alignment="se">
<m>1</m>
</point>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two curves above the x axis, with the points zero and one marked on the axis. The curve f 1 of x equals x cubed plus one, drawn in blue, rises from one to two across the interval from zero to one. The curve f 2 of x equals negative two x squared plus two x, drawn in vermillion, is an arch from zero up to one half and back to zero. Nothing between them is shaded: the region is left for the reader to draw.">
  <annotation ref="c1-f1" text="The curve f 1 of x equals x cubed plus 1, drawn in blue."/>
  <annotation ref="c1-f2" text="The curve f 2 of x equals negative 2 x squared plus 2 x, drawn in vermillion."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-general-regions-case2">
<title>Case II: Regions of the Form <m>R = \{(x,y) : c \le y \le d,\ g_1(y) \le x \le g_2(y)\}</m></title>

<example xml:id="example-case2-horizontal">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the following double integral,
</p>

<me>
\iint_R x^2 y\,dx\,dy
</me>

<p>
where
<m>R = \{(x,y) : 0.1 \le y \le 0.5,\ y^2 \le x \le y^{1/3}\}</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for computing the double integral of Example II.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-case2-region-prefigure">
<caption>
The graphs of <m>x = g_1(y) = y^2</m> and <m>x = g_2(y) = y^{1/3}</m>.
Shade the region <m>R</m> of <xref ref="example-case2-horizontal"/> on
them, and draw a typical strip.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>
The graphs of a left and a right curve, with the region between them left to shade.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-case2-region">
<diagram dimensions="(380,300)" margins="10">
<definition>g1(t) = (t^2, t)</definition>
<definition>g2(t) = (t^(1/3), t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.12,-0.09,1.22,0.68)">
<axes at="c2-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="c2-g1" function="g1" domain="(0,0.62)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="c2-g2" function="g2" domain="(0,0.62)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<label at="c2-g1-label" anchor="(0.02,0.56)" alignment="ne"
    color="#D55E00"><m>g_1(y) = y^2</m></label>
<label at="c2-g2-label" anchor="(0.72,0.22)" alignment="se"
    color="#0072B2"><m>g_2(y) = y^{1/3}</m></label>
<point at="c2-y1" p="(0,0.1)" fill="black" alignment="w">
<m>0.1</m>
</point>
<point at="c2-y5" p="(0,0.5)" fill="black" alignment="w">
<m>0.5</m>
</point>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two curves leaving the origin, with the points zero point one and zero point five marked on the y axis. The curve x equals g 1 of y equals y squared, drawn in vermillion, hugs the y axis. The curve x equals g 2 of y equals the cube root of y, drawn in blue, sweeps out to the right. Nothing between them is shaded: the region is left for the reader to draw.">
  <annotation ref="c2-g1" text="The curve x equals g 1 of y equals y squared, drawn in vermillion."/>
  <annotation ref="c2-g2" text="The curve x equals g 2 of y equals y to the one third, drawn in blue."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The idea behind both cases<mdash/>sweeping a strip across the
region while the inner integral measures the length of the
strip<mdash/>is animated in <xref ref="fig-strip-sweep-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-strip-sweep-video">
<caption>
Animation of iterated integration over a general region. For the
region of <xref ref="example-case1-vertical"/>, a vertical strip at
position <m>x</m> has height <m>f_1(x) - f_2(x)</m>; as the strip
sweeps from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 1</m>, the outer integral
accumulates the strip contributions. Use it to check your answer to
that example.
</caption>
<video preview="play-button.png" xml:id="video-strip-sweep"
       source="videos/strip_sweep_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-tetrahedron-volume">
<title>Volume of a Tetrahedron</title>

<example xml:id="example-tetrahedron">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the volume of the tetrahedron bounded by the planes
<m>x + 2y + z = 2</m>, <m>x = 2y</m>, <m>x = 0</m> and <m>z = 0</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,12.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for finding the volume of the tetrahedron.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-tetrahedron-tikz">
<caption>
The tetrahedron <m>T</m> bounded by the planes <m>x + 2y + z = 2</m>
(slanted face), <m>x = 2y</m> (vertical face through the
<m>z</m>-axis), <m>x = 0</m> (the <m>yz</m>-plane) and <m>z = 0</m>
(the base). Its vertices are <m>(0,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,0,2)</m>,
<m>(0,1,0)</m> and <m>\left(1,\tfrac12,0\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<shortdescription>
A tetrahedron with one vertex at the origin, bounded by a slanted plane and a vertical plane.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a tetrahedron drawn in translucent
blue. One vertex is at the origin, one at height two on the z axis,
one at zero one zero on the y axis, and one at the point one comma one
half comma zero. The slanted face x plus two y plus z equals two and
the vertical face x equals two y are labeled with arrows.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={97}{20},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=1.5, ymin=0, ymax=1.5, zmin=0, zmax=2.4,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % vertices: O(0,0,0), A(0,0,2), B(0,1,0), C(1,0.5,0)
    % ---- back face x = 0 : O, A, B ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.15]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:0,1,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- base z = 0 : O, B, C ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.22]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,1,0) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- face x = 2y : O, A, C ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.32]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- slanted face x + 2y + z = 2 : A, B, C ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.45]
      (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:0,1,0) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- edges ----
    \draw[oiblue!70!black, line width=1.0pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,2)
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,1,0)
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0)
      (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:0,1,0)
      (axis cs:0,0,2) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0)
      (axis cs:0,1,0) -- (axis cs:1,0.5,0);
    % ---- vertices and labels ----
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.6pt, color=black,
        forget plot] coordinates {(0,0,2) (0,1,0) (1,0.5,0)};
    \node[anchor=west, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.02,0.06,2.05)
      {$(0,0,2)$};
    \node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:0,1.03,0.08)
      {$(0,1,0)$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\small] at (axis cs:1.05,0.5,-0.1)
      {$\left(1,\tfrac12,0\right)$};
    \node[anchor=south east, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.02,-0.04,-0.02)
      {$0$};
    \node[font=\large] at (axis cs:0.28,0.55,0.35) {$T$};
    % ---- face labels with arrows ----
    \draw[-{Latex}, black!70, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0.75,0.15,1.4) -- (axis cs:0.42,0.35,0.9);
    \node[anchor=south, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.75,0.15,1.42)
      {$x = 2y$};
    \draw[-{Latex}, black!70, line width=0.7pt]
      (axis cs:0.45,1.05,1.35) -- (axis cs:0.3,0.62,1.0);
    \node[anchor=south, font=\small] at (axis cs:0.45,1.05,1.37)
      {$x + 2y + z = 2$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-tetrahedron-base-prefigure">
<caption>
Sketch the region of integration for <xref ref="example-tetrahedron"/>:
the base of the tetrahedron in the <m>xy</m>-plane, with the lines that
bound it and a typical vertical strip.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=2.2, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-0.25,-0.25) grid[step=0.25] (1.5,1.25);
\draw[->, thick] (-0.25,0) -- (1.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-0.25) -- (0,1.35) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {0.5,1} \draw (\x,-0.03) -- (\x,0.03) node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {0.5,1} \draw (-0.03,\y) -- (0.03,\y) node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid, marked at one half and one on each axis, for sketching the base of the tetrahedron and a vertical strip.</shortdescription>
</image>
</figure>

<remark xml:id="remark-reverse-order">
<title>Why not integrate in the reverse order?</title>
<p>
We could also try to integrate with respect to <m>x</m> first, using
horizontal strips. However, as the figure below shows, the right end of
a horizontal strip depends on where the strip is: for
<m>y \le \tfrac12</m> the strip ends on the line <m>x = 2y</m>, while
for <m>y \ge \tfrac12</m> it ends on the line <m>x + 2y = 2</m> (that
is, <m>x = 2 - 2y</m>). We would therefore have to split the region
into two pieces and evaluate two double integrals, so integrating with
respect to <m>y</m> first is the more efficient choice here.
</p>

<sidebyside width="60%">
<image>
<shortdescription>
The same triangle swept by horizontal arrows for the reversed order of integration.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-tetrahedron-base-reverse">
<diagram dimensions="(420,300)" margins="10">
<definition>top(t) = (t, 1 - t/2)</definition>
<definition>bot(t) = (t, t/2)</definition>
<definition>topsc(t) = 1 - t/2</definition>
<definition>botsc(t) = t/2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.4,2.3,1.55)">
<axes at="rv-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<area-between-curves at="rv-region" function1="botsc" function2="topsc"
    domain="(0,1)" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none"/>
<vector at="rv-strip1" v="(1.35,0)" tail="(-0.4,0.3)" stroke="#D55E00"
    thickness="3"/>
<vector at="rv-strip2" v="(1.25,0)" tail="(-0.4,0.75)" stroke="#CC79A7"
    thickness="3"/>
<line at="rv-split" endpoints="((0,0.5),(1.15,0.5))" stroke="black"
    thickness="2" dash="6 4"/>
<parametric-curve at="rv-top" function="top" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="rv-bot" function="bot" domain="(0,1)"
    stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<line at="rv-left" endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))" stroke="#555555"
    thickness="3"/>
<point at="rv-end1" p="(0.6,0.3)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="rv-end2" p="(0.5,0.75)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="rv-corner" p="(1,0.5)" fill="black" alignment="e">
<m>\left(1,\tfrac12\right)</m>
</point>
<label at="rv-split-label" anchor="(0.06,0.47)" alignment="se">
<m>y = \tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="rv-top-label" anchor="(0.02,1.18)" alignment="ne"
    color="#0072B2"><m>x = 2 - 2y</m></label>
<label at="rv-bot-label" anchor="(0.72,0.2)" alignment="se"
    color="#009E73"><m>x = 2y</m></label>
<label at="rv-origin" anchor="(-0.06,-0.06)" alignment="sw">
<m>0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same triangular region with two horizontal arrows. A vermillion arrow at height y equals zero point three crosses the region and its right end lies on the green line x equals two y. A reddish purple arrow at height y equals zero point seven five crosses the region and its right end lies on the blue line x equals two minus two y. A dashed line at y equals one half separates the two cases.">
  <annotation ref="rv-strip1" text="A horizontal strip below y equals one half. Its right end lies on the line x equals 2 y."/>
  <annotation ref="rv-strip2" text="A horizontal strip above y equals one half. Its right end lies on the line x equals 2 minus 2 y."/>
  <annotation ref="rv-split" text="The dashed line y equals one half where the right boundary changes from one line to the other, forcing a split into two integrals."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</remark>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-area-by-double-integration">
<title>Area by Double Integration</title>

<p>
In the previous session, we talked about finding the double integral
<xref ref="eq-double-integral-def"/> and how it corresponds to the
volume of an object with base <m>R</m> and height <m>z = f(x,y)</m>.
This session, we will look at a special case of this double integral,
which corresponds to the area of region <m>R</m>:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-area-double-integral">
\text{Area of region } R = \iint_R dx\,dy = \iint_R dA.
</men>

<example xml:id="example-area-double-integration">
<title>Example IV</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the area of the region <m>R</m> shown in
<xref ref="fig-area-region-prefigure"/>, bounded on the right by
<m>x = y - y^3</m>, on the left by <m>x = -1</m>, and by the parabola
<m>y = (x+1)^2</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em> <alert>Method 1.</alert>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,12.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for Method 1.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
<alert>Method 2.</alert>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,7.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for Method 2.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-area-symmetry-prefigure">
<caption>
Method 2: the region of <xref ref="example-area-double-integration"/>
drawn with the unit square <m>[-1,0]\times[-1,0]</m>, the strip between
the parabola <m>y = (x+1)^2</m> and the <m>y</m>-axis, and the two
pieces marked <m>A</m>.
</caption>
<image width="65%">
<shortdescription>
The region decomposed into a unit square and two matching pieces.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-area-symmetry">
<diagram dimensions="(360,320)" margins="10">
<definition>right(t) = (t - t^3, t)</definition>
<definition>leftR2(t) = (sqrt(t) - 1, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.7,-1.45,1.1,1.55)">
<axes at="as-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<rectangle at="as-square" lower-left="(-1,-1)" dimensions="(1,1)"
    stroke="none" fill="#f4e3c1"/>
<polygon at="as-lobeA" fill="#f5c6bc" stroke="none" closed="yes"
    points="((0,0),(-0.04159,-0.04167),(-0.08275,-0.08333),(-0.123,-0.125),(-0.162,-0.1667),(-0.1993,-0.2083),(-0.2344,-0.25),(-0.2669,-0.2917),(-0.2963,-0.3333),(-0.3223,-0.375),(-0.3443,-0.4167),(-0.3621,-0.4583),(-0.375,-0.5),(-0.3827,-0.5417),(-0.3848,-0.5833),(-0.3809,-0.625),(-0.3704,-0.6667),(-0.3529,-0.7083),(-0.3281,-0.75),(-0.2955,-0.7917),(-0.2546,-0.8333),(-0.2051,-0.875),(-0.1464,-0.9167),(-0.0782,-0.9583),(0,-1))"/>
<polygon at="as-lobeB" fill="#f5c6bc" stroke="none" closed="yes"
