<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A region in the plane split into two parts. R 1, shaded tan, lies between y equals negative one and y equals zero, bounded on the left by the vertical line x equals negative one and on the right by the curve x equals y minus y cubed. R 2, shaded light green, lies between y equals zero and y equals one, bounded on the left by the parabola y equals the square of x plus one and on the right by the same curve x equals y minus y cubed.">
  <annotation ref="ar-right" text="The right boundary of both subregions: the cubic curve x equals y minus y cubed, drawn in blue."/>
  <annotation ref="ar-left2" text="The left boundary of R 2: the parabola y equals the square of x plus 1, drawn in vermillion, which solved for x is x equals square root of y minus 1."/>
  <annotation ref="ar-left1" text="The left boundary of R 1: the vertical line x equals negative 1."/>
  <annotation ref="ar-r1" text="The subregion R 1, below the x axis, shaded tan."/>
  <annotation ref="ar-r2" text="The subregion R 2, above the x axis, shaded light green."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-general-regions-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
Both problems below ask only for a <em>setup</em>: a double integral whose
value is the volume or the area in question, with a region of integration
that is no longer a rectangle. Each figure can be rotated by dragging the
background, and zoomed by scrolling.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-di-cylinder-sphere">
<title>A ball cut by a cylinder</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a double integral for computing the volume of the region bounded by
the cylinder <m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m> and the sphere
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m>. See <xref ref="fig-ws-cylinder-sphere"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for setting up the volume integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-ws-cylinder-sphere">
<caption>
Figure (4): the sphere <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m> and the cylinder
<m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m>. Use this graph to find the region of integration
and the integrand.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="z7qbvdyz"
             width="100%" aspect="500:500" design-width="500"
             material-width="500" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture and scroll to zoom. The black
curve is where the sphere and the cylinder meet.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-di-area-arcsin">
<title>Problem: an area as a double integral</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a double integral for computing the area of the region bounded by
the blue curve and the red curve, which are graphed in
<xref ref="fig-ws-arcsin-region"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,11.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for setting up the area integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-ws-arcsin-region">
<caption>
Figure (5): the region bounded by the blue arc of the circle
<m>x^2+y^2=1+\frac{\pi^2}{4}</m> and the red curve
<m>y=\sin^{-1}(x)</m>. The two curves meet at
<m>\left(1,\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m> and
<m>\left(-1,-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m>; the dashed arc is the rest of
the circle.
</caption>
<image width="64%">
<shortdescription>
A tilted circle with solid and dashed halves bounding the shaded region.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-arcsin-region">
<diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="10">
<definition>R = sqrt(1 + pi^2/4)</definition>
<definition>arcsine(u) = (sin(u), u)</definition>
<definition>circ(t) = (R*cos(t), R*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.5,-2.5,2.5,2.5)">
<polygon at="ar-region" closed="yes" fill="#dcecf7" stroke="none"
    points="((-1.0000,-1.5708),(-0.9968,-1.4902),(-0.9871,-1.4097),(-0.9709,-1.3291),(-0.9485,-1.2486),(-0.9200,-1.1680),(-0.8855,-1.0875),(-0.8452,-1.0069),(-0.7994,-0.9264),(-0.7485,-0.8458),(-0.6927,-0.7653),(-0.6324,-0.6847),(-0.5681,-0.6042),(-0.5000,-0.5236),(-0.4287,-0.4430),(-0.3546,-0.3625),(-0.2782,-0.2819),(-0.2000,-0.2014),(-0.1205,-0.1208),(-0.0403,-0.0403),(0.0403,0.0403),(0.1205,0.1208),(0.2000,0.2014),(0.2782,0.2819),(0.3546,0.3625),(0.4287,0.4430),(0.5000,0.5236),(0.5681,0.6042),(0.6324,0.6847),(0.6927,0.7653),(0.7485,0.8458),(0.7994,0.9264),(0.8452,1.0069),(0.8855,1.0875),(0.9200,1.1680),(0.9485,1.2486),(0.9709,1.3291),(0.9871,1.4097),(0.9968,1.4902),(1.0000,1.5708),(1.0000,1.5708),(0.9150,1.6218),(0.8274,1.6682),(0.7374,1.7099),(0.6454,1.7467),(0.5515,1.7786),(0.4560,1.8054),(0.3593,1.8271),(0.2616,1.8436),(0.1631,1.8549),(0.0641,1.8610),(-0.0350,1.8618),(-0.1341,1.8573),(-0.2327,1.8475),(-0.3307,1.8325),(-0.4278,1.8123),(-0.5236,1.7870),(-0.6180,1.7566),(-0.7106,1.7212),(-0.8012,1.6809),(-0.8895,1.6359),(-0.9753,1.5862),(-1.0584,1.5321),(-1.1384,1.4736),(-1.2152,1.4109),(-1.2886,1.3442),(-1.3583,1.2737),(-1.4242,1.1996),(-1.4860,1.1221),(-1.5436,1.0415),(-1.5969,0.9578),(-1.6456,0.8715),(-1.6896,0.7827),(-1.7289,0.6916),(-1.7632,0.5986),(-1.7926,0.5039),(-1.8169,0.4078),(-1.8360,0.3105),(-1.8499,0.2124),(-1.8586,0.1136),(-1.8620,0.0146),(-1.8602,-0.0846),(-1.8530,-0.1835),(-1.8406,-0.2818),(-1.8230,-0.3794),(-1.8003,-0.4759),(-1.7724,-0.5710),(-1.7395,-0.6645),(-1.7017,-0.7562),(-1.6590,-0.8457),(-1.6116,-0.9328),(-1.5597,-1.0172),(-1.5034,-1.0988),(-1.4427,-1.1772),(-1.3780,-1.2524),(-1.3094,-1.3239),(-1.2371,-1.3917),(-1.1613,-1.4556),(-1.0822,-1.5153),(-1.0000,-1.5708))"/>
<axes at="ar-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="ar-dashed" function="circ" domain="(4.14548,7.28707)"
    stroke="#7f7f7f" thickness="2" dash="6 5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ar-arc" function="circ" domain="(1.00388,4.14548)"
    stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="ar-curve" function="arcsine" domain="(-pi/2,pi/2)"
    stroke="#CC0000" thickness="3"/>
<point at="ar-top" p="(1,pi/2)" fill="black" alignment="ne">
<m>\left(1,\tfrac{\pi}{2}\right)</m>
</point>
<point at="ar-bottom" p="(-1,-pi/2)" fill="black" alignment="sw">
<m>\left(-1,-\tfrac{\pi}{2}\right)</m>
</point>
<label at="ar-curve-label" anchor="(0.30,-1.30)" alignment="e"
    color="#CC0000"><m>y=\sin^{-1}(x)</m></label>
<label at="ar-arc-label" anchor="(-1.55,1.02)" alignment="e"
    color="#0072B2"><m>x^2+y^2=1+\tfrac{\pi^2}{4}</m></label>
<label at="ar-region-label" anchor="(-0.85,0.35)" alignment="center">
<m>D</m>
</label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A circle centered at the origin of radius the square root of one plus pi squared over four. The upper left half of the circle is drawn as a solid blue arc and the lower right half as a dashed grey arc. A red S-shaped curve, the graph of y equals inverse sine of x, runs from the point negative one, negative pi over two, through the origin, up to the point one, pi over two; those two points are the ends of the blue arc and are opposite ends of a diameter. The region between the red curve and the blue arc is shaded and labelled D. Its area is half the area of the disk.">
  <annotation ref="ar-region" text="The shaded region D, bounded on the right by the red curve and on the left and top by the blue arc."/>
  <annotation ref="ar-curve" text="The red curve y equals inverse sine of x, running from negative one, negative pi over two through the origin to one, pi over two."/>
  <annotation ref="ar-arc" text="The blue arc, half of the circle of radius the square root of one plus pi squared over four, running counterclockwise from one, pi over two to negative one, negative pi over two."/>
  <annotation ref="ar-dashed" text="The dashed grey arc completing the circle."/>
  <annotation ref="ar-top" text="The upper meeting point of the two curves, at one, pi over two."/>
  <annotation ref="ar-bottom" text="The lower meeting point of the two curves, at negative one, negative pi over two."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
